Fathom-Spite
Ingredients: THRASH METAL + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + Double Bass + 2010s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The genre apes the call-and-response of container ships greeting each other in fog.

Fathom-Spite is a savage strain of dockside crossover thrash driven by breakneck tempos, class-war vitriol, and an unorthodox low-end: a savagely distorted, slapped, and bowed acoustic double bass. Razor-sharp 2010s palm-muted guitar attacks surge into blinding speed runs, only to stop dead for booming, low-frequency acoustic groans and gut-string clatter that answer the guitar squeals like lost ocean liners hailing one another through harbor mist.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 190–235 BPM. Rapid-fire crossover thrash skate-beats and galloping double-kicks collide violently with sudden, cavernous half-time anchor drops where the double bass takes over the pulse.
- Harmonic Palette: Locrian and Phrygian modes laden with abrasive tritones, drop-D dissonance, and low open-string double bass drones vibrating in sub-audible fundamental frequencies.
- Production Signatures: 
  - Dry-Slap / Wet-Sub Split: The upright double bass is mic'd with a clicky bridge pickup running through a dirty Rat pedal alongside an internal body mic doused in five-second plate reverb to emulate distant maritime foghorns.
  - The Fog Gate: Abrupt full-band mutes where all tracks hard-gate out except for a decaying high-register guitar feedback loop, immediately answered in the opposite stereo channel by a roaring contrabass swell.
  - Steel-Hull Room Compression: Drum overheads run through extreme brickwall limiting inside a concrete or metal room profile to deliver the claustrophobic slap of an empty cargo hold.
- Dynamics: Bipolar whiplash. Tracks detonate into blinding thrash-riff speed runs, collapse instantly into eerie ambient gaps filled only by bowed acoustic bass groan and tube-amp sizzle, and then detonate back into unison, gang-shouted fury.

Scene & Origin Lore
Fathom-Spite congealed between 2012 and 2015 along the freezing, automated container terminals of Rotterdam and Port Newark. Born out of wildcat strikes and logistics-worker squats, youth facing hyper-mechanized austerity rejected standard four-string electric bass guitars as too thin and sterile, instead dragging battered orchestral double basses into derelict warehouses. Wearing grease-blackened high-visibility vests over thrash band hoodies, oilskin jackets, and steel-toed boots, crews gathered in empty corrugated freight containers to hold illegal, generator-powered gigs. The central ritual of the scene was "The Hail": guitarists would abruptly kill their volume knobs mid-mosh, leaving the bassist to draw an overdriven bow across the lowest gut string in a deafening low-register drone, which the crowd answered with synchronized, bellowing throat-roars before the pit detonated back into a 220-BPM frenzy.

Typical Instruments: Upright acoustic double bass (gut or steel strings, piezo pickups, bowed and slapped through tube bass stacks), high-output electric guitars (tuned to Drop D, fast alternate-picking, solid-state boost into tube heads), acoustic drum kit (steel snare tuned ping-tight, rapid double-bass pedals), shouted/barked hardcore-thrash vocals, handheld air horns, industrial hydrophone samples.

For Fans Of: Power Trip, Municipal Waste, Colin Stetson, The Goddamn Gallows

